    "id": "train-502",
    "question": "At a garage sale, Tish bought 4 items: a red horseshoe magnet, two stuffed animals, and a large sticker which read, \"Why can't teachers solve their own math problems?\"  The magnet cost three times more than the sticker, but only one quarter the price of the two stuffed animals combined.  If the Magnet cost $3, how much, in dollars, did a single stuffed animal cost?",
    "gold": "6",
    "answer": "If the magnet cost 3-times more than the sticker, the sticker cost 3/1=1 dollar.\nIf the magnet cost 1/4 the price of the two stuffed animals combined, then two stuffed animals cost 3*4=12 dollars.\nThus, a single stuffed animal cost 12/2=<<12/2=6>>6 dollars.\n#### 6"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the cost of a single stuffed animal, we start by defining the cost of the magnet, the sticker, and the two stuffed animals. Let's denote the cost of the magnet as \\( M \\), the cost of the sticker as \\( S \\), and the cost of one stuffed animal as \\( A \\).\n\nFrom the problem, we know:\n1. The magnet cost $3, so \\( M = 3 \\).\n2. The magnet cost three times more than the sticker, so \\( M = 3S \\).\n3. The magnet cost one quarter the price of the two stuffed animals combined, so \\( M = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 2A \\).\n\nFirst, we use the information that the magnet cost three times more than the sticker:\n\\[ M = 3S \\]\nSince \\( M = 3 \\), we substitute 3 for \\( M \\):\n\\[ 3 = 3S \\]\nSolving for \\( S \\), we get:\n\\[ S = 1 \\]\n\nNext, we use the information that the magnet cost one quarter the price of the two stuffed animals combined:\n\\[ M = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 2A \\]\nSince \\( M = 3 \\), we substitute 3 for \\( M \\):\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 2A \\]\nSimplifying the right side of the equation, we get:\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{2A}{4} \\]\n\\[ 3 = \\frac{A}{2} \\]\nSolving for \\( A \\), we multiply both sides by 2:\n\\[ A = 6 \\]\n\nTherefore, the cost of a single stuffed animal is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
